1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,560
 Alright, the time has come to talk about our very first domain.

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:09,280
 This week we're going to be looking at translating mechanical systems.

3
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:13,960
 As the name suggests, this week we're going to be studying bodies which move by only translating,

4
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:16,640
 that is to say, not rotating.

5
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,760
 And when you reduce the body down to something which only translates, it's size and its

6
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:23,360
 shape become unimportant.

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:26,680
 You might be familiar with this concept, you might have seen it in physics before, we're

8
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:29,200
 treating these bodies as point masses.

9
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,040
 So all of the characteristics of that body, whether it's sharp edged or rounded, whether

10
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:37,680
 it takes up a lot of space or not very much space, these things are unimportant for

11
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:38,680
 our analysis.

12
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:43,000
 Unless, of course, there's a CCR which depends on that that might impose some sort of

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,440
 force on the body, maybe something like air resistance.

14
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:50,920
 This week we're going to restrict our analysis to rectilinear motion, one demotion.

15
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:55,120
 But to extend this technique to systems which move in more than one dimension, all you have

16
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:59,040
 to do is model each dimension the same way that we do for the one dimension we're going

17
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:00,480
 to look at this week.

18
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:06,560
 As a side note, things which move in two dimensions, but do so along a kind of fixed path,

19
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:07,800
 work in the same way.

20
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,880
 And many of these techniques can be extended to work on things in path coordinates, for

21
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:13,200
 example.

22
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:18,160
 So I guess the very first thing we need to talk about is how you choose states, the fundamental

23
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:23,440
 variables that are going to characterize our systems for translating mechanical systems.

24
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:27,240
 I told you at the end of the mass spring damper video that in general, this was going

25
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:31,480
 to be a complicated process and that I would introduce you to rules of thumb for each domain.

26
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:35,320
 Well, the time has come for one of these rules of thumb and here it is.

27
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:40,920
 For translating mechanical systems, we require twice as many states as we have degrees of freedom

28
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:42,960
 present in the system.

29
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,360
 And generally speaking, we're going to choose the momenta of the masses that we're dealing

30
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:48,920
 with and their positions.

31
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:52,800
 All the deformations of any attached springs as our states.

32
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:57,320
 Now this is in always the case and I'll show you a counter example to this later this

33
00:01:57,320 --> 00:01:58,320
 week.

34
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:01,920
 But as a starting point, at first blush, this is a pretty good approach.

35
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:06,800
 Now depending on whether or not you've done courses in mechanics or whether you've done

36
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:11,280
 a computer aided design course, you may or may not be familiar with the concept of a degree

37
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:12,520
 of freedom.

38
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:14,280
 And I'll put this as simply as I can.

39
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:19,000
 A degree of freedom is simply an independent way that a mass can move.

40
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,760
 Unconstrained bodies in the real world have six degrees of freedom.

41
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:29,280
 They can translate about x, y, and z, and they can rotate about each of these axes,

42
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:33,080
 x, y, and z.

43
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:37,000
 Sometimes things are constrained in a way that limits their degree of freedom.

44
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,880
 So for example, this week we're looking at things which translate in only one direction,

45
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:47,120
 which means that in general, every disconnected mass has one degree of freedom.

46
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,920
 But of course, if a mass can move in two different directions, x and y independently,

47
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:53,840
 it would have two degrees of freedom.

48
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:58,440
 And if I had something like two masses that were rigidly linked so that as long as I knew

49
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:03,040
 the position of one, I could write the position of the other and some sort of static function

50
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:06,480
 of that first one, that would only be one degree of freedom.

51
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:08,800
 They act like one mass.

52
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:13,480
 So just to sum up, you need twice as many states as you have degrees of freedom.

53
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:18,280
 And in general, at least for rectilinear motion, you have one degree of freedom for each disconnected

54
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:22,840
 mass, meaning for every disconnected mass you need two states.

55
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:28,400
 Generally, these two states will look something like the momentum of that mass and its position,

56
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:33,400
 either directly or described by the deformation of the spring, which is just how to do it.

57
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:37,520
 Let's have a go at problem 2.1 and see if we can work out how many degrees of freedom

58
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:41,480
 and how many different states we're going to need to model a bunch of mechanical systems.
